<%= UiComponents::Lookbook::TableOfContents.call(page) %>

Wires a label, description and error message to one control, and validates it with the browser's own constraints.
[Appica docs](https://appica.dev/ui/components/react/field)

<%= embed UI::FieldPreview, :default %>

## Usage

```ruby
Field(name: "user[email]", error: @user.errors[:email].to_sentence.presence, validation_mode: :blur,
      messages: { value_missing: "Email is required", type_mismatch: "Enter a valid email address" }) do
  FieldLabel { "Email" }
  Input(type: "email", required: true, value: @user.email)
  FieldDescription { "We'll send your receipt here." }
  FieldError()
end
```

The control inside - an [Input](<%= page_path(:input) %>), a [Textarea](<%= page_path(:textarea) %>) - takes the
field's id, `name:`, `aria-describedby` and its disabled and invalid state, so the parts need no ids.

Validation uses the control's native constraints (`required`, `type`, `pattern`, `minlength`, `min`/`max`…).
`validation_mode:` picks when they are checked: on the form's `:submit` (the default - an invalid field stops the
submit and takes focus), on `:blur` or on every `:change`. Once invalid, the field re-checks as you type and clears
as soon as the value is valid. The message is the browser's unless `messages:` has one for the failure.

Server errors come in as `error:`: the field renders invalid with that message, so a re-rendered form shows it
without JavaScript.

